The dependencies are $u \to v,w,t$, $w \to x,y$ and $z \to x$ (checked with attribute closures by script: $u^+=\{u,v,w,t,x,y\}$, $w^+=\{w,x,y\}$, $z^+=\{z,x\}$). A set of relations in 3NF that "uses all the dependencies" has one relation per determinant, with the determinant as key:

- $D1(\underline{u}, v, w, t)$ for $u \to v,w,t$;
- $D2(\underline{w}, x, y)$ for $w \to x,y$ ($w$ is the foreign key linking D1 to D2);
- $D3(\underline{z}, x)$ for $z \to x$.

This is option B. The other options fail:

- A is one big relation: with key $(u,z)$ it contains partial and transitive dependencies, so it is not in 3NF;
- C loses the dependency $w \to x$ (D2 keeps only $y$) and D3 has the key $(w,z)$ although $z$ alone determines $x$, a partial dependency;
- D also loses $u \to w$ (D1 has no $w$), so $u$ is no longer linked to $w$, and its D3 has the same partial dependency.

**Answer: B.**

